# Create slides with AI and TSX

Create a project, keep its preview open, and ask Claude Code to edit `deck.tsx`.
Saving the source rebuilds the presentation. The browser is a **view-only slide
viewer**: vertical thumbnails, a selected slide, zoom, and presentation mode.
All content and layout changes happen in TSX.

## 1. Set up the tools

You need **Node.js 22.18 or later**, Git, and **pnpm 10.34.4** for the source
checkout. Claude Code is optional; any editor can change the same TSX files.

**The TSX packages are not yet published to npm.** The core library's npm
package alone does not install this workflow. Use the source checkout below;
`npx @office-kit/pptx-dev` will not work until the companion packages are published.

Run these commands in a terminal (macOS/Linux shell):

```sh
git clone https://github.com/office-kit/pptx.git
cd pptx
pnpm install --frozen-lockfile
pnpm --filter @office-kit/pptx-dev... build
```

If you already have this repository, start in its root and run the last two
commands. Package the four local libraries so your slide project can live outside
the repository. Run this in the same terminal:

```sh
PPTX_REPO="$PWD"
PPTX_PACKS="$PPTX_REPO/.probe/packages"
mkdir -p "$PPTX_PACKS"
pnpm --filter @office-kit/pptx-dev... exec pnpm pack --pack-destination "$PPTX_PACKS"
```

## 2. Create your presentation project

Choose a new directory. `init` refuses to overwrite an existing one. Here we
create `my-slides` beside the repository:

```sh
node "$PPTX_REPO/packages/dev/dist/cli.mjs" init ../my-slides
cd ../my-slides
npm install --legacy-peer-deps "$PPTX_PACKS"/*.tgz
npm run check
npm run dev
```

The temporary `--legacy-peer-deps` option is needed for this source-checkout
installation: the DSL requires the next core release, while the checkout's core
version number has not been bumped yet. Install **all four tarballs from the same
checkout**; its core includes the required changes. This option is not a general
recommendation for installing released packages. Keep the tarballs available if
you need to reinstall this project. After updating the checkout, use an empty
pack directory so each package has only one tarball version.

The terminal prints a URL such as `http://127.0.0.1:4173`. Open the **printed
URL** and leave the process running. You should see one cover slide with the
heading “Your next presentation”. If that port is busy, use
`npm run dev -- --port 4174`.

Your project contains:

| File | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `deck.tsx` | The presentation source. Ask the AI to edit this file. |
| `CLAUDE.md` | Instructions for the AI: units, elements, templates, and verification. |
| `tsconfig.json` | TSX runtime configuration, type checking, and editor completion. |
| `.vscode/tasks.json` | Tasks for preview, checking, and export. |
| `package.json` | The `dev`, `check`, and `build` commands. |

## 3. Ask Claude Code to create the slides

Open **another terminal in `my-slides`** and start your installed Claude Code:

```sh
claude
```

For example, give it this request:

```text
Read CLAUDE.md and edit deck.tsx to create a five-slide presentation in Japanese
for a quarterly business review: cover, summary, revenue chart, challenges,
and next actions. Use a white background, dark text and an orange accent.
Use clearly labelled sample data where figures have not been supplied.
Use native Text, Shape, Chart and Table elements, not screenshots of slides.
Run npm run check and npm run build after making changes.
```

Keep the preview beside the conversation. After inspecting the result, ask for
specific changes such as “Make the chart on slide 3 larger and shorten its
headline”. The AI edits TSX; the viewer updates after each save and keeps the
selected slide index. If a build fails, an error appears above the last successful
preview. Ask the AI to fix that error before continuing.

The viewer does not contain an AI chat. Claude Code runs in your terminal or
editor. Automatic rebuilding also does **not** run TypeScript checking; use
`npm run check` to catch type errors.

## 4. Review and export

- Select slides in the left thumbnail strip; use arrow keys, Page Up/Down,
  Home, or End to navigate.
- Use **Fit** or a zoom percentage to inspect the slide.
- Use **Present** for presentation mode. Arrow keys, Space, or clicking the
  slide advances; Shift+Space goes back; Escape exits. If the browser denies
  fullscreen, presentation mode stays inside the window.
- There are no text editors, drag handles, resizing, or slide-reordering controls.
  Ask the AI to make those changes in TSX.

When ready, run in the project directory:

```sh
npm run check
npm run build
```

The output is **`my-slides/deck.pptx`**. **Download PPTX** in the viewer also saves
the last successful build. If an error is visible, that download is the older
successful version, not the failing source.

Text, shapes, tables and charts are native PPTX objects. The preview is rendered
from the exported PPTX, but it does not guarantee pixel-identical PowerPoint
rendering or animation/media playback. Check the exported file in the target
application before delivering it.

## Use VSCode beside the viewer

Open the `my-slides` folder in VSCode. Completion and diagnostics come from the
generated `tsconfig.json`; no React or Vue installation is needed.

Run **Tasks: Run Task → Preview presentation**, then **Simple Browser: Show**
and paste the printed URL. Move the browser editor to a side group. Alternatively,
keep an ordinary browser window beside VSCode. Use only one preview process for
this project; skip the task if `npm run dev` is already running in a terminal.

A dedicated VSCode extension and click-to-source navigation are not implemented.

## Start from an existing PPTX

Copy the file into `my-slides/template.pptx`, then inspect it:

```sh
npx --no-install office-pptx inspect template.pptx
```

Use the returned names or IDs as targets. Replace `deck.tsx` with this example,
changing `Title 1` to an actual shape name from your file:

```tsx
import { readFile } from 'node:fs/promises';
import { Presentation, Slide, Fill } from '@office-kit/pptx-dsl';

const source = await readFile(new URL('./template.pptx', import.meta.url));

export default (
  <Presentation source={source} mode="edit">
    <Slide target={{ index: 0 }}>
      <Fill target={{ name: 'Title 1' }}>Quarterly business review</Fill>
    </Slide>
  </Presentation>
);
```

`mode="edit"` preserves the existing slide sequence and changes only explicit
targets. Indices are zero-based. Existing masters, layouts and unknown package
parts are retained through the core model, rather than recreated from the DSL's
known fields. Replacing text intentionally replaces that target's text.

Use `mode="compose"` only when you want to replace the original slide sequence.
Keep imported components, templates and images inside the project directory so
the watcher can detect their changes. See the
<a href="{base}/docs/tsx">TSX elements and template guide</a> for the syntax.

## Troubleshooting

| Symptom | What to do |
| --- | --- |
| npm reports 404 for `@office-kit/pptx-dev` or `@office-kit/pptx-dsl` | These packages are not published yet. Follow the local tarball installation above. |
| `office-pptx` is not found | Install the project dependencies and run `npm run dev` inside `my-slides`. |
| An import or JSX type cannot be found | Check that dependencies are installed and VSCode opened the generated project folder with its `tsconfig.json`. |
| An asset cannot be found | Load it with `readFile(new URL('./asset.png', import.meta.url))`, relative to the source file. |
| Saving an asset does not rebuild | Keep it under the entry file's directory; `node_modules`, `.git`, `dist` and `.office-kit` are excluded. |
| A template target is missing or ambiguous | Run `inspect`, then choose an exact shape ID, name, or slide part name. |

For AI-readable documentation, append `.md` to this page's URL, or use
<a href="{base}/llms-full.txt">the full documentation text</a>.
